    "display_name": "Hurricane Maria",
    "bubble_choice_description": "Practice using pandas functions to identify key temperature statistics and uncover initial insights.",
    "thumbnail_url": "https://images.code.org/6e7efa33cd1b673c12742673565104db-hurricane.png",
    "teacher_markdown": "**What Students Should Take Away:**\r\n- Students practice using the min() and max() functions to identify key temperature extremes in the dataset.\r\n- Students learn how to apply the sum() function to calculate total sea surface temperatures, helping them understand cumulative changes.\r\n- Students reinforce their skills in using the agg() function combined with groupby() to summarize data, providing insights into overall trends.\r\n\r\n**Misconceptions Students Might Have or Points They Might Struggle With and Tips to Assist:**\r\n- Students might think that only one statistic (e.g., mean) is important, neglecting the significance of other statistics.\r\n  - Tip: Encourage students to consider how each statistic offers a different perspective. For instance, the mean shows overall trends, while min and max highlight extremes.\r\n- Students may have difficulty interpreting how temperature changes relate to the progression of the hurricane.\r\n  - Tip: Guide students to think about how rising or falling temperatures could indicate changes in the hurricane's intensity. Use guiding questions to help them connect the data to real-world events.",
